ECN as quic-go's ecnTracker marks and validates it, in place of upstream's validation.

quic-go (vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/ecn.go) marks the first ten 1-RTT packets ECT(0) — any
of them, ACK-only and path MTU probes included — then marks nothing until an acknowledgement of a
testing packet shows the marks arrived, and after that marks every 1-RTT packet; it has no deadline.
Its verdicts are its own: more ECT(0) counted than sent, marked packets acknowledged with no counts,
counts that go down, fewer new ECT(0)+CE than marked packets newly acknowledged, every testing packet
lost, or every one lost or CE — failed, for good. Upstream ngtcp2 differed in what a client shows:

  - it tested with ack-eliciting packets only: an ACK-only packet was marked only once the path was
    capable, and a path MTU probe never during the test;
  - it gave up after three PTOs, marked nothing from then on unless the acknowledgements proved the
    path, and counted datagrams rather than packets;
  - its checks were its own (any ECN-acknowledged packet made the path capable; a plain ACK for a
    marked packet failed it), taken after the ACK's losses rather than before;
  - it started over on Retry and on a new path; quic-go never does.

The marking of a datagram is chosen by the first packet written into it, as quic-go asks ECNMode once
per datagram, IsOnlyShortHeaderPacket deciding (vendor/quic-go/connection.go:2578, :2637,
:2895): a long header packet in front makes it Not-ECT to the end. The ACK-only datagram written
before the handshake is confirmed is Not-ECT, as quic-go writes it with ECNMode(false) (:2705-2707).
A CONNECTION_CLOSE takes the mark and is not counted: quic-go never registers it (:2895-2897).

ngtcp2_rtb keeps no packet that elicits no acknowledgement and carries no padding, and quic-go's
history does, until one is acknowledged or declared lost (internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:
331, :590-626, :843-888). The tracker counts those among the newly acknowledged, so they are kept
beside it (nae) and given up by quic-go's two thresholds. An ACK that newly acknowledges nothing is not
handed to the tracker and does not raise the largest acknowledged it compares against (:424-427,
:452-458).

Checked against quic-go's own ecnTracker, run by tools/qgovec: test/ngpatch.

